WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.412
A running total is another classic use of window
functions The row_revenue

00:00:05.425 --> 00:00:10.200
expression multiplies quantity by price for each
sale row The windowed SUM

00:00:10.225 --> 00:00:13.175
then adds revenue from the beginning of the ordered
data through the

00:00:13.188 --> 00:00:18.725
current row ORDER BY sale_date and sale_id inside
OVER defines

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:24.188
the calculation sequence The explicit ROWS BETWEEN
UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.900
frame means start at the first row in the window and
include

00:00:26.925 --> 00:00:31.950
everything through the current row As you move down
the result running_revenue

00:00:31.975 --> 00:00:36.400
should never reset because there is no PARTITION BY
Each new sale

00:00:36.412 --> 00:00:40.825
adds to the previous cumulative amount The final
ORDER BY uses the

00:00:40.838 --> 00:00:44.000
same date and sale ID so the displayed rows follow
the sequence

00:00:44.025 --> 00:00:48.175
used in the calculation Run the query and verify the
first few

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:52.938
rows manually The first running total should equal
the first row revenue

00:00:53.812 --> 00:00:57.100
The second running total should equal the first plus
the second and

00:00:57.125 --> 00:01:02.700
so on Running totals are useful for cumulative sales
account balances inventory

00:01:02.712 --> 00:01:06.112
movement progress tracking and many time-oriented
analyses
